    "FOLLOW-UP": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "PATIENT MONITORING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "canonical_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Physical exam, thoracic radiographs, lymph node evaluation, ± abdominal ultrasound at 1–2 months, then every 3 months after treatment (complete surgical excision).",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS.",
          "canonical_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Complete surgical excision of the primary lesion and no evidence of metastasis; additional treatment may not be required.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Survival time following complete surgical excision depends upon location of the tumor on the digit; squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) originating from subungual epithelium: 95% 1-year and 74% 2-year survival; SCC originating in other parts of the digit: 60% 1-year and 44% 2-year survival.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In one study 1- and 2-year survival was 50% and 18%, respectively, while in two other studies only 20–45% of dogs died of SCC (multicentric or metastatic) and the median survival time was not reached.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Development of multicentric disease (multiple affected digits) in dogs appears more common than lymph node or pulmonary metastasis.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Surgery to amputate affected digit, regardless of presence of metastases, provides positive impact on survival in the dog.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Histologic grading does not appear predictive of development of multicentric or metastatic disease in dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Prognosis for cats is poor with median survival times of 2–3 months if metastatic from pulmonary carcinoma.",
              "pdf_page": 1365,
              "printed_page": 1283,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
